  "narrative": "All newly delivered estimates are signed to the claim direction, so positive values support the claimed direction and negative values oppose it. The feature_019 interactions do not support scientifically meaningful effect modification in this dataset. H52 and H55 have positive signed points only about +0.120 and +0.121, below the 0.15-unit threshold, with intervals crossing zero and including the below-threshold region; H53 is far below threshold; H54 is opposite the claimed direction. Also, feature_019 analyses are restricted to the 1,556 observed values, so nonrandom missingness could bias the comparisons. Therefore reject and close H52-H55. The four final binary screens are also unsupported: H56 is opposite/near-zero, H57 and H59 are essentially null, and H58 is opposite/near-zero; all intervals include zero and fail the effect-size standard, so reject and close H56-H59. The most important new results are H60-H63, conditional simple effects decomposing the two largest marginal associations. H60 is signed +0.809 with interval [0.678, 0.940], and H61 is signed +0.832 with interval [0.771, 0.893]; both correspond to raw negative feature_037 associations, i.e. stronger dependency, within feature_002=1 and feature_002=0 respectively. H62 is signed +0.845 with interval [0.714, 0.976], and H63 is signed +0.822 with interval [0.761, 0.882]; both correspond to raw positive feature_002 associations, i.e. weaker dependency, within feature_037=1 and feature_037=0 respectively. All four exceed the 0.15-unit threshold decisively and have lower bounds far above threshold, so accept them as dataset-specific conditional associations. However, they are not evidence of interaction or amplification: earlier interaction tests for the same predictor pairs were rejected, and these are simple effects within contexts. Small exposed cells in H60 and H62 (n=79) and lack of confounder adjustment remain limitations; the magnitude is large enough that ordinary multiplicity concerns are unlikely to explain all four, but the claims remain associations in the supplied dataset only. I request validation of H38, the accepted main feature_010 association, because it is one of the core accepted predictors but remains unvalidated, and the new conditional feature_037/feature_002 context analyses do not independently test it.",
  "research_notes": "Current supported dataset-specific associations: H13 feature_037=1 vs 0 raw negative about -0.83, validated, stronger dependency; H21 feature_063=1 vs 0 raw negative about -0.23 to -0.28, validated with modest threshold-boundary certainty, stronger dependency; H37 feature_002=1 vs 0 raw positive about +0.83, weaker dependency; H38 feature_010=1 vs 0 raw positive about +0.42, weaker dependency, now selected for independent validation; H46/H47 feature_002 positive within feature_010=1/0; H60/H61 feature_037 negative within feature_002=1/0; H62/H63 feature_002 positive within feature_037=1/0. New negative results: H52-H55 feature_019>=60 interactions unsupported, including because feature_019 has missingness and analyses use only observed feature_019; H56-H59 final binary screens unsupported. The accepted simple effects show that the largest associations persist across opposite binary contexts but do not establish interaction or causal effect modification. Multiplicity is high overall, but the strong accepted contrasts are far above threshold, while all near-threshold interaction/screening contrasts failed. No causal interpretation, leakage, or generalizability is claimed. If H38 validates, it strengthens the accepted main-effect set; if it fails or is imprecise, reassess feature_010 as dataset-specific only and consider validating H37 instead if budget remains. Further broad binary/categorical screening has mostly exhausted useful untested nominal predictors; future work should focus on assessing feature_010 validation and possibly a final independent check of the remaining unvalidated large main effect H37 rather than adding more weak interaction screens.",
